DataDirectory /app/data/tor
UseBridges 1

# Tor 0.4.8+ default Conflux + the default NumPrimaryGuards 3 are exactly
# what we want: with the 3 bridges start.sh requires, every onion-service
# circuit gets the 2-link-disjoint Conflux pair, halving cold-query latency
# vs single-circuit. (Earlier images set ConfluxEnabled 0 / NumPrimaryGuards 2
# to make 2-bridge configs survive — that workaround is gone now that the
# image requires 3 bridges.)

# SOCKS proxy: explicit loopback bind; HAProxy connects here for SOCKS4 routing.
SocksPort 127.0.0.1:9050
# No ControlPort — no authenticated management channel is exposed.

ClientTransportPlugin obfs4 exec /usr/bin/lyrebird

# Cloudflare's hidden DNS resolver returns this RFC1918 sentinel IP when
# clients query it; we MapAddress it back to the .onion before connecting.
# The rewrite is purely client-side — nothing leaks to the network.
MapAddress 10.192.0.1 dns4torpnlfs2ifuz2s2yf3fc7rdmsbhm6rw75euj35pac6ap25zgqad.onion

# Tor 0.4.9+ rejects SOCKS connections to RFC1918 destinations BEFORE applying
# MapAddress, so haproxy's `socks4 127.0.0.1:9050` target of 10.192.0.1:853 is
# refused with "SOCKS4 Proxy deny the request" even though MapAddress would
# rewrite it to the onion. Disable the early reject for the SOCKS path; the
# only RFC1918 destination we ever connect to here is 10.192.0.1, which the
# MapAddress above redirects to a .onion — nothing escapes the loopback.
ClientRejectInternalAddresses 0

# Connection-warmth tuning for the long-lived haproxy → .onion DoT path.
#
# KeepalivePeriod 60 — send padding cells every 60s on idle circuits so the
#   guard/middle relays don't tear them down for inactivity. Without this,
#   between haproxy health checks (every 15s after our fix, but a single
#   client query can leave the backend conn idle for the dnsmasq cache TTL),
#   Tor may close the underlying circuit and the next query pays a fresh
#   rendezvous build (5-15s) instead of reusing the warm circuit.
#
# CircuitsAvailableTimeout 3600 — keep pre-built circuits in the pool for an
#   hour. Default is 30 min; with a DNS-only workload most pre-builts go
#   unused, but the few we *do* use should stay warm across idle gaps.
#
# MaxCircuitDirtiness 3600 — recycle each circuit's streams every hour.
#   This was 600, noted as "longer than 10 min would gain warmth at
#   meaningful anonymity cost". That tradeoff has been deliberately
#   re-taken, because the cost of the 10-min window proved larger than
#   assumed: for hidden services this is measured from the circuit's *last*
#   use, so a DNS-only workload retires the rendezvous circuit after 10 idle
#   minutes and the next query pays a full rebuild.
#
#   Benchmarked on nice-dns over the same 20 domains, fresh unbound cache
#   per variant, run forward and reversed to control for drift. The two
#   configurations carrying this setting were the only ones that stayed fast
#   in both orderings:
#
#     MaxCircuitDirtiness 3600   median  998ms / 1013ms
#     default 600 (baseline)     median 1730ms / 3675ms, p90 up to 9750ms,
#                                max up to 30s
#
#   The anonymity cost is unchanged in kind and now larger in degree:
#   streams to the same .onion from one client over one circuit are
#   correlatable, and this widens that window from 10 minutes to an hour.
#   It is an explicit choice for warmth, not an oversight.
KeepalivePeriod 60
CircuitsAvailableTimeout 3600
MaxCircuitDirtiness 3600

# ConfluxClientUX latency — the Conflux note at the top of this file relies
#   on two link-disjoint rendezvous circuits being paired. Conflux defaults
#   to "throughput", which optimises bulk transfer; DNS is the opposite
#   workload. "latency" selects the MinRTT scheduler, which per proposal 329
#   "works by always and only sending on the circuit with the current
#   minimum RTT".
#
#   Honest status: this did NOT survive benchmarking. It looked like a ~45%
#   median win running early in a sequence (932ms vs 1730ms baseline), then
#   measured 2198ms when the same config ran late in the reversed run — it
#   was tracking network drift, not configuration. Kept because the
#   mechanism suits this workload and it costs nothing, not because it was
#   measured to help.
ConfluxClientUX latency

# LongLivedPorts 853 — "circuits for streams that use these ports will
#   contain only high-uptime nodes", and the manual notes the list "is also
#   honored for circuits (both client and service side) involving hidden
#   services whose virtual port is in this list". Our DoT onion is reached
#   on virtual port 853, which is absent from the default list
#   (21, 22, 706, 1863, ...). Biases the rendezvous circuit toward relays
#   less likely to vanish mid-session — the event that forces a rebuild.
#
#   Honest status: inconclusive. It targets stall *frequency* rather than
#   per-query latency, which a 20-sample benchmark cannot resolve; one run
#   measured 1383ms and the other failed to bootstrap at all.
LongLivedPorts 853
